  "description": "A quick win: understand Light Therapy and Clock Resetting.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "Doctors now prescribe light itself as medicine. A bright lamp at the right time can reset a broken clock.",
      "visual": "💡"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Light therapy uses timed exposure to bright, blue-enriched light to shift the circadian rhythm forward or backward.",
      "visual": "🔦"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "Morning light advances the clock, making you sleepy earlier. Evening light delays it, pushing sleep later.",
      "visual": "🌅"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "Just 30 minutes of 10,000-lux light in the morning can relieve seasonal depression as effectively as medication.",
      "visual": "☀️"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Astronauts on the International Space Station use special LED panels to simulate sunrise and prevent circadian chaos.",
      "visual": "🚀"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "Light is the most powerful drug for your circadian clock, and it requires no prescription.",
      "visual": "🌤️"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What effect does morning bright light have on the circadian clock?",
    "options": [
      "It advances the clock, making you sleepy earlier at night",
      "It delays the clock, keeping you awake longer",
      "It has no measurable effect on circadian timing"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
